      },
      {
        "name": "The Gilt Stamp Rule",
        "body": "Gilt marks; it never carries. Use it for stamped meta (dates, categories, scripture references), column headings on bookcloth, and small marks like the crest and count chips. Never for body text, button fills, or button labels.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Margin Is Structure Rule",
        "body": "Margin red exists only as the two hairlines of the double margin. It never colours text, icons, or fills; errors use `error`, live status uses `live`.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Figures Rule",
        "body": "Every number that can sit in a column or be compared (counts, amounts, dates, times, page numbers, stat values, pagination summaries) is set with tabular lining figures. Tables get it by default; everything else takes the `num` utility. Money columns are right-aligned.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Slab Speaks, Sans Reads Rule",
        "body": "Bitter is for headings, titles, and display figures. Anything a person reads in sentences is Public Sans.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Rows Before Cards Rule",
        "body": "Lists of entries (events, service times, audit entries, messages, footer links) are ruled rows, not card grids. Cards are for items where a thumbnail carries meaning: sermons, gallery, leaders, roles.",
